([CASE STUDY 2: Reverse Thinking Exercise on Cybersecurity Training]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem Context: Corporate employees routinely clicked on simulated phishing emails) Tj
T*
(despite mandatory 2-hour video compliance lectures.) Tj
T*
(Reverse Thinking Prompt: 'How might we guarantee that EVERY employee clicks malicious) Tj
T*
(phishing links and leaks passwords immediately?') Tj
T*
(Generated 'Worst' Ideas:) Tj
T*
(1. Send emails offering free company merchandise or bonuses that look urgently official.) Tj
T*
(2. Make cybersecurity lectures so mind-numbingly boring that employees put videos on) Tj
T*
(mute and tab away.) Tj
T*
(3. Punish employees who report suspicious emails by making them fill out 10-page) Tj
T*
(bureaucratic incident forms.) Tj
T*
(Inverted Innovative Solutions:) Tj
T*
(- Inversion of 1: Train employees on deceptive emotional urgency triggers through) Tj
T*
(30-second weekly gamified micro-challenges.) Tj
T*
(- Inversion of 2: Replace 2-hour compliance videos with interactive simulated) Tj
T*
(role-playing games where employees play the role of the hacker.) Tj
T*
(- Inversion of 3: Introduce a one-click 'Report Phishing' button in Outlook that) Tj
T*
(instantly rewards reporters with corporate recognition badges.) Tj
T*
(Measurable Results: Phishing click rates dropped from 28% to 1.4% within three months.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
